const e=[{id:"sv-1-161",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The famous figure of **3.4 defects per million opportunities** for a “Six Sigma process” rests on which assumption?",options:["The process mean is allowed to drift 1.5σ in the long term, leaving 4.5σ between the mean and the nearest specification limit","The process stays perfectly centred with 6σ between the mean and each specification limit, with no long-term drift","It counts 3.4 defective units per million units produced, whatever the number of opportunities per unit","The defects are split evenly over both tails: 1.7 below the LSL and 1.7 above the USL"],answer:0,hint:"Six Sigma short-term capability minus the conventional 1.5σ long-term shift gives the long-term Z used for the 3.4 DPMO figure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A 6σ process (short-term) is assumed to shift by **1.5σ** over the long term. The nearest specification limit is then $6-1.5=4.5$ standard deviations from the mean, and the one-tailed area beyond $Z=4.5$ is about 3.4 per million."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only the first option states the 1.5σ shift and the 4.5σ distance to the **nearest** limit. A perfectly centred 6σ process would give about 0.002 DPMO (two parts per billion) — far fewer than 3.4."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The figure is per million **opportunities** (defects), not per million units (defectives), and it is one-tailed: after the shift almost all defects sit beyond one limit."}],final:"3.4 DPMO = one tail beyond Z = 4.5 after a 1.5σ shift → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A centred 6σ process with no drift gives about 0.002 DPMO, not 3.4; this ignores the 1.5σ shift.","This confuses defects with defectives: DPMO counts defects per million opportunities, not defective units per million units.","After the 1.5σ shift the defects fall almost entirely beyond the nearer limit — the figure is one-tailed, not split over two tails."],trap:"Short-term 6σ ↔ long-term 4.5σ ↔ 3.4 DPMO: one tail, defects, opportunities.",bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-1-162",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which **Motorola engineer** is widely credited as the “father of Six Sigma” for proposing the approach in the mid-1980s?",options:["Jack Welch","W. Edwards Deming","Bill Smith","Taiichi Ohno"],answer:2,hint:"Six Sigma started at Motorola in the 1980s; GE popularised it in the 1990s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Six Sigma was established at **Motorola**. Engineer **Bill Smith** proposed it in 1986 as a way to cut defects, and the method was then developed further at Motorola (with Mikel Harry among others)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"**Jack Welch** made Six Sigma famous as GE’s company-wide strategy from 1995 — he did not create it. **Deming** is linked to PDSA and his 14 points; **Ohno** to the Toyota Production System (Lean)."}],final:"Bill Smith, Motorola, 1986 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Jack Welch (GE CEO) popularised Six Sigma from 1995; he was not a Motorola engineer and did not originate it.","Deming is a quality pioneer (PDSA, 14 points), not the Motorola originator of Six Sigma.","","Taiichi Ohno developed the Toyota Production System — the root of Lean, not Six Sigma."],trap:"Created at Motorola (Bill Smith) ≠ made famous at GE (Jack Welch).",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-163",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"At which DMAIC tollgate is the team expected to show **statistically verified root causes** — the vital few X’s **proven** to affect Y?",options:["Define","Measure","Analyze","Improve"],answer:2,hint:"Measure lists and screens potential X’s; Analyze proves or disproves them with data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The roadmap’s Analyze step is “Prove/Disprove the impact X’s have on the problem”. Hypothesis tests and regression turn **suspected** X’s into **verified** X’s."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The word **verified** points to the Analyze tollgate. In Measure the team builds the fishbone, X-Y matrix and FMEA, which give a **prioritised list of potential** X’s — not proof."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Define hands over the charter, CTQs and SIPOC. Improve uses the verified X’s to find, pilot and optimise solutions."}],final:"Verified critical X’s are an Analyze deliverable → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Define delivers the charter, CTQs, SIPOC and COPQ estimate — no causes are tested yet.","Measure identifies and prioritises potential X’s (fishbone, X-Y matrix, FMEA) but does not statistically prove them.","","Improve starts from the verified X’s and develops solutions; proving the causes should already be done."],trap:"“Potential” or “prioritised” X’s = Measure; “verified” or “proven” X’s = Analyze.",bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-164",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A field-service team fits this model:

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: FirstTimeFix versus TechTenure, JobsPerDay, PartsAvail

Regression Equation
FirstTimeFix = 61.2 + 1.85 TechTenure - 2.40 JobsPerDay + 0.21 PartsAvail
\`\`\`

In **Y = f(X)** terms, which variable is the **Y**?`,options:["TechTenure","JobsPerDay","The constant 61.2","FirstTimeFix"],answer:3,hint:"Y is the response (output) on the left of the equation; the X’s are the predictors listed after “versus”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Y = f(X), **Y** is the output (dependent variable, response) and the **X’s** are the inputs (independent variables, predictors)."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Minitab writes “**Response versus Predictors**” and puts the response alone on the left of the equation: **FirstTimeFix**. TechTenure, JobsPerDay and PartsAvail are the X’s."}],final:"Y = FirstTimeFix (the response) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["TechTenure is the first predictor listed after “versus” — an X, not the Y.","JobsPerDay has the largest coefficient in size, but it is still a predictor (X); the size of a coefficient does not make it the output.","61.2 is the constant (intercept), the predicted Y when all X’s are zero — not a variable.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 208"},{id:"sv-1-165",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In **Y = f(X)**, the X’s are called the **dependent** variables because they depend on the output Y. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"The output depends on the inputs, not the other way round.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Y** is the **dependent** variable (output, effect, symptom). The **X’s** are the **independent** variables (inputs, causes) that drive Y."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"The statement reverses the relationship. Y depends on the X’s, so the statement is **False**."}],final:"X’s are independent, Y is dependent → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["This reverses the model: Y is the dependent variable; the X’s are independent inputs that determine Y.",""],trap:"Dependent = Y = output; independent = X = input.",bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114"},{id:"sv-1-166",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt draws a chart of daily average answer time with **±3σ control limits** calculated from 25 days of data. Beside it she marks the **60-second answer target** written into the enterprise customer’s SLA. Which pairing is correct?",options:["Control limits = Voice of the Customer; 60-second target = Voice of the Process","Control limits = Voice of the Process; 60-second target = Voice of the Customer","Both are the Voice of the Process, because both are drawn on the same chart","Control limits = Voice of the Business; 60-second target = Voice of the Employee"],answer:1,hint:"Control limits come from the process data; specification limits and targets come from the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits are calculated from the process’s own variation — they are the **Voice of the Process (VOP)**. Specification limits and service targets express what the customer requires — the **Voice of the Customer (VOC)**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The ±3σ limits come from 25 days of data → VOP. The 60-second target comes from the customer’s SLA → VOC."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Comparing the two is exactly what capability does: does the VOP fit inside the VOC?"}],final:"Control limits = VOP, SLA target = VOC → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This swaps the two: control limits are calculated from process data, so they cannot be the customer’s voice.","","Being on the same chart does not make the SLA a process voice; it was set by the customer, not calculated from the data.","Neither element comes from the business’s financial goals or from employees; the limits are VOP and the target is VOC."],trap:"Control limits ≠ specification limits.",bookRef:"Book pp. 112, 525"},{id:"sv-1-167",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An operator wants a **full-time** improvement specialist who leads complex, cross-functional DMAIC projects under a Champion’s direction and **coaches Green Belts** on their projects. Which role is this?",options:["Green Belt","Master Black Belt","Process Owner","Black Belt"],answer:3,hint:"Match full-time vs part-time, and project leader vs programme-level technical expert.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines a **Black Belt** as a full-time Six Sigma team leader (about four weeks of training) who solves problems under the direction of a Champion. Leading the larger projects and supporting Green Belts is typical of the role."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Full-time + leads complex projects under a Champion + coaches Green Belts → **Black Belt**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A **Green Belt** is part-time and runs smaller projects in their own area. A **Master Black Belt** has training beyond Black Belt and teaches and mentors the Belts across the deployment. A **Process Owner** runs the process day to day."}],final:"Full-time project leader under a Champion → Black Belt → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Green Belt is part-time, working on smaller-scope projects in their own area and supporting Black Belt projects.","A Master Black Belt is the deployment-level technical expert who teaches and mentors Black Belts — beyond the project-leader role described.","The Process Owner is responsible for running the process and its performance, not for leading DMAIC projects as a specialist.",""],trap:"Black Belt leads projects; Master Black Belt coaches the Belts and the programme.",bookRef:"Book pp. 584, 586"},{id:"sv-1-168",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Enterprise orders pass between **Sales, Credit Control, Provisioning and Field Operations**. The Champion wants one map that shows **which department performs each step** and **every hand-off** between departments. Which map should the Green Belt draw?",options:["SIPOC diagram","Swim-lane (deployment) process map","Spaghetti diagram","Value stream map"],answer:1,hint:"One lane per department or role; each crossing between lanes is a hand-off.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **swim-lane (deployment-flow) map** puts each department or role in its own lane, so it shows who is responsible for each step. Every time work crosses a lane there is a supplier–customer hand-off."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The Champion wants “who does each step” and “every hand-off” → swim-lane map."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SIPOC is a high-level scoping tool with no lanes. A spaghetti diagram traces physical movement. A value stream map focuses on flow, inventory and time (VA vs NVA)."}],final:"Responsibilities and hand-offs → swim-lane map → answer **B**",whyWrong:["SIPOC lists suppliers, inputs, 4–7 high-level steps, outputs and customers; it does not show who does each step or the hand-offs.","","A spaghetti diagram traces the physical path of people or material on a layout, not departmental responsibility.","A value stream map shows material and information flow with lead time and inventory; it is not designed to show which department owns each step."],bookRef:"Book p. 103"},{id:"sv-1-169",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Instant eSIM activation once delighted mobile customers. Now that every competitor offers it, customers simply **assume** it: they are angry if it is missing but get **no extra satisfaction** when it is there. In the **Kano model**, what has the feature become?",options:["A basic (must-be) requirement","A performance (one-dimensional) requirement","An indifferent attribute","A reverse attribute"],answer:0,hint:"Kano: delighters drift over time towards expected requirements.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kano categories: **basic (must-be)** — expected; missing causes dissatisfaction, present adds nothing. **Performance** — more is better, in a straight line. **Delighter** — unexpected, adds satisfaction. Over time delighters become expected."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Angry if missing, no extra satisfaction when present” is the textbook description of a **basic (must-be)** requirement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A performance requirement would raise satisfaction as it improves (faster is better). Indifferent attributes cause no reaction either way; reverse attributes annoy when present."}],final:"Expected, only noticed when absent → basic (must-be) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A performance requirement raises satisfaction as it gets better; here presence adds nothing, which is the must-be pattern.","Indifferent means customers do not care either way — but here they are angry when it is missing.","A reverse attribute lowers satisfaction when present; customers want this feature."],trap:"Today’s delighter is tomorrow’s must-be.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.2"},{id:"sv-1-170",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`The CTQ for the business help line is “**each call answered within 30 seconds**”. Last month’s data:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Within30s

Within30s  Count  Percent
Yes          316    79.00
No            84    21.00
N=           400

Descriptive Statistics: AnswerTime (s)

Variable     N   Mean  StDev  Median
AnswerTime 400   22.6   14.1    18.0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["The CTQ is met, because the mean answer time of 22.6 s is below 30 s","Each call is judged against the CTQ: 84 calls (21%) are defects, so the CTQ is not being met reliably","Half the calls meet the CTQ, because the median is 18 s","Every call fails, because the standard deviation of 14.1 s is larger than the allowed margin"],answer:1,hint:"A CTQ with a specification is judged unit by unit: each unit outside the spec is a defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ sets a requirement for **each** call. Customers feel the individual call, not the average; performance is the share of units inside the specification."},{title:"Read the output",body:"The Tally shows **84 of 400 calls (21%)** took longer than 30 s. Those are defects, even though the mean (22.6 s) is below 30 s."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A mean below the limit can hide a long right tail. The median says half the calls took 18 s or less — not that half met the CTQ. The standard deviation alone does not decide pass or fail."}],final:"21% of calls exceed 30 s → CTQ not met reliably → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Judging a per-unit CTQ by the average hides the 21% of calls above 30 s — the mean can be inside the spec while many units are outside it.","","The median (18 s) means half the calls took 18 s or less; it does not mean only half met 30 s — the Tally shows 79% did.","A standard deviation is not a pass/fail rule; 79% of calls were inside the CTQ, so “every call fails” contradicts the Tally."],trap:"Mean inside the spec ≠ customers satisfied.",bookRef:"Book pp. 112, 509"},{id:"sv-1-171",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A team develops CTQs for a new business-broadband offer. Put these steps in order:

- **P** Validate the CTQs with the customer
- **Q** Identify the customer
- **R** Translate the needs into measurable CTQs with specifications
- **S** Capture the Voice of the Customer`,options:["S → Q → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:3,hint:"Book: identify the customer → capture the VOC → … → validate the CTQs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists the steps in developing CTQs as **identifying the customer**, **capturing the Voice of the Customer** and finally **validating the CTQs**. Translating needs into measurable CTQs sits between capture and validation."},{title:"Apply to the letters",body:"Q (identify customer) → S (capture VOC) → R (translate into CTQs) → P (validate with customer)."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **D**",whyWrong:["You cannot capture the Voice of the Customer before you know who the customer is.","Needs cannot be translated into CTQs before the Voice of the Customer has been captured.","Validation must check finished CTQs; it cannot come before the needs are translated into measurable CTQs.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-172",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does the term **hidden factory** describe?",options:["Rework, re-checks and other non-value-added work needed to produce good output, which normal reporting does not recognise as waste","A subcontracted facility used for overflow work during demand peaks","Process capacity deliberately held in reserve for unexpected demand","Customer requirements that were never captured during the VOC work"],answer:0,hint:"It is the corrective work that no report shows — part of the cost of poor quality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines the **hidden factory** as corrective and non-value-added work needed to produce a unit of output that is generally **not recognised** as waste of resources, materials and cost."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only the first option describes unrecognised rework and re-checking. It is “hidden” because final-yield figures show only what finally passed, not the effort spent fixing it."}],final:"Unrecognised rework and NVA effort → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A subcontracted overflow facility is a visible, contracted resource, not unrecognised corrective work.","Reserve capacity is a planning choice; the hidden factory is the waste of rework and re-checks.","Missed customer requirements are a VOC gap, not the hidden rework inside the process."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-173",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A service unit totals one year of quality-related costs:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Cost category; Sum of Cost (OMR)

Category              Sum   Percent
Prevention          9,000      2.5
Appraisal          63,000     17.5
Internal failure  126,000     35.0
External failure  162,000     45.0
All               360,000    100.0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["Cut appraisal first: inspection adds no value, so removing it lowers total cost straight away","Only the external failure cost of OMR 162,000 counts as COPQ; the rest is normal running cost","Failure costs make up 80% while prevention gets only 2.5%; shifting effort into prevention should lower failure (and later appraisal) costs","Prevention spending should be cut, because it is part of the cost of quality and gives no direct return"],answer:2,hint:"Prevention is cheapest per defect; failure costs (internal + external) are the biggest target.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Internal + external failure = 35.0% + 45.0% = **80%** of the total. Prevention is only **2.5%**."},{title:"Key idea",body:"Money spent on **prevention** stops defects being made, which reduces both internal and external failure costs. Once defects are rarer, less appraisal is needed."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Removing inspection without preventing defects lets them reach customers, so external failure grows. Internal failure (rework, scrap) is also COPQ."}],final:"Failure 80%, prevention 2.5% → invest in prevention → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Cutting appraisal without preventing defects lets more defects escape to customers, so external failure cost — already the largest — would rise.","Internal failure costs (rework, scrap, re-tests) are also COPQ; this keeps only one of the two failure categories.","","Prevention is the cheapest category and the one that reduces failure costs; cutting it moves cost in the wrong direction."],trap:"Spend on prevention to save on failure — not the other way round.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-174",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt charts **600** escalations to second-line support:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Escalation Reason

Reason    Access  Config  Billing  Hardware  Power  Other
Count        112     104       98        96     94     96
Percent     18.7    17.3     16.3      16.0   15.7   16.0
Cum %       18.7    36.0     52.3      68.3   84.0  100.0
\`\`\`

What is the BEST next step?`,options:["Start on Access, because it is the tallest bar","Work on the first four reasons, because together they reach about 68%","Conclude that escalations are random and stop the Pareto analysis","Re-stratify the data by another factor (site, shift, product or customer type), or weight the categories by cost, to look for a vital few"],answer:3,hint:"A useful Pareto chart shows a few tall bars; a flat chart means the current split hides the pattern.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"All six bars sit between 15.7% and 18.7%. It takes five of six categories to pass 80%, so there is **no vital few** in this split."},{title:"Key idea",body:"A flat Pareto tells you the data are cut along the wrong dimension, not that the causes are random. Stratify by another factor (site, shift, product), or weight by cost or impact, and chart again."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Picking the top bar would cover less than a fifth of the problem. Four categories reaching 68% is not a vital few — it is nearly everything."}],final:"Flat Pareto → re-stratify or weight by cost → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Access is only 18.7% — barely above the others — so fixing it alone leaves over 80% of escalations untouched.","Four of six categories is most of the list, not a vital few; this applies the 80:20 rule mechanically to a flat chart.","A flat chart says this way of grouping hides the pattern; it does not prove the causes are random.",""],trap:"No vital few? Change the stratification, not the rule.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-175",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"On a Pareto chart, categories are ordered from largest to smallest, and a combined **“Other”** category is placed at the far right **even if it is larger** than some of the named categories. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Named categories are sorted; the catch-all group always goes last.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart sorts the named categories in descending order. The small categories combined into “Other” are always shown **last**, because “Other” is not a single cause to act on."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"Minitab’s Pareto chart does exactly this, so the statement is **True**."}],final:"Sorted bars, “Other” always last → **True** (answer **A**)",whyWrong:["","“Other” is a mix of small causes, not one actionable category, so it stays at the right end even when its total is larger than some named bars."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-176",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for building a Pareto chart in order:

- **P** Sort the categories from largest to smallest
- **Q** Define the categories and the measure (count or cost) and collect the data
- **R** Draw the bars and the cumulative-percentage line
- **S** Calculate each category’s percentage and the cumulative percentage`,options:["Q → S → R → P","P → Q → S → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:2,hint:"Data first, then sort, then cumulative percentages, then the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"You need data before you can sort it, and you must sort before cumulative percentages make sense, because the cumulative line adds the categories from largest to smallest."},{title:"Apply to the letters",body:"Q (define and collect) → P (sort descending) → S (percent and cumulative percent) → R (draw bars and line)."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Cumulative percentages worked out before sorting give a wrong cumulative line, and sorting cannot come after the chart is drawn.","You cannot sort categories before they are defined and counted.","","The cumulative-percentage line cannot be drawn before the cumulative percentages are calculated."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-177",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An eKYC team checks **2,400** online application forms. Each form has **6** defect opportunities. The check finds **36 defects**, spread over **30 forms**. What is the **DPMO**?",options:["2,083","2,500","12,500","15,000"],answer:1,hint:"DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit) × 1,000,000.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$D=36$ defects, $U=2400$ forms, $O=6$ opportunities per form. (30 is the number of defective forms — not needed for DPMO.)"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{U\\times O}\\times 10^{6}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{36}{2400\\times 6}\\times 10^{6}=\\dfrac{36}{14{,}400}\\times 10^{6}=2{,}500$$"}],final:"DPMO = 2,500 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["2,083 uses the 30 defective forms instead of the 36 defects (defectives ≠ defects).","","12,500 is defective forms per million forms (30 ÷ 2,400 × 10⁶) — a PPM-defective figure, not DPMO.","15,000 forgets the opportunities: 36 ÷ 2,400 × 10⁶ is defects per million units (DPU × 10⁶)."],trap:"DPMO uses defects and opportunities; the count of defective units is a distractor.",bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-178",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A fault-triage desk runs **two 8-hour shifts** a day. Each shift has **60 minutes** of planned breaks. Customers raise **280** tickets a day. What is the **takt time**?",options:["0.33 min","1.50 min","3.00 min","3.43 min"],answer:2,hint:"Takt time = net available working time ÷ customer demand in the same period.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Each shift: $480-60=420$ net minutes; two shifts give $2\\times 420=840$ minutes. Demand = 280 tickets a day."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{Net available time}}{\\text{Customer demand}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{840}{280}=3.00\\text{ min per ticket}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The desk must finish one ticket every **3 minutes** to keep pace with demand."}],final:"Takt = 840 ÷ 280 = 3.00 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.33 is the formula upside down (280 ÷ 840) — tickets per minute, not minutes per ticket.","1.50 uses only one shift’s 420 minutes against the whole day’s demand.","","3.43 uses gross time (960 min) without removing the planned breaks."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-179",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"An auditor re-checks **500** SIM-swap requests. One request that was already recorded with **1 defect** turns out to have **3 more** defects. Nothing else changes. Which metric will **NOT** change?",options:["DPU","DPMO","DPO","Proportion of defective requests (% defective)"],answer:3,hint:"Defect-based metrics count every defect; defective-based metrics count each faulty unit only once.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defect** is a single failure to meet a requirement; a **defective** is a unit with one or more defects. DPU, DPO and DPMO count **defects**; % defective counts **units**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The total defect count rises by 3, so DPU, DPO and DPMO all rise. The request was already defective, so the number of defective requests is unchanged and **% defective stays the same**."}],final:"Only the defective-unit metric is unchanged → answer **D**",whyWrong:["DPU = defects ÷ units; three extra defects raise the numerator, so DPU rises.","DPMO is based on total defects, so it rises with the three extra defects.","DPO = defects ÷ opportunities; it rises for the same reason as DPMO.",""],trap:"Defects can pile up on one unit; a unit is defective only once.",bookRef:"Book pp. 585, 587"},{id:"sv-1-180",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A deployment lead screens four candidate problems. Which one is BEST handled as a **“just do it”** action rather than chartered as a DMAIC project?",options:["The NOC’s shared printer jams daily; the vendor has confirmed a worn feed roller, and replacing it is a 1-hour job","22% of billing disputes are reopened, and nobody knows why","Fibre activation time varies widely between regions for no known reason","Repeat field visits have risen for six months, with several suspected but unproven causes"],answer:0,hint:"DMAIC fits recurring, measurable problems with unknown root causes; known cause + known fix = just do it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A DMAIC project is worth its effort when the problem is recurring and measurable and the **root cause is unknown**. When cause and fix are already known and the fix is cheap and low-risk, simply implement it."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"The printer has a confirmed cause (worn roller) and a 1-hour fix → **just do it**. The other three have unknown or unproven causes, which is what DMAIC is for."}],final:"Known cause, known quick fix → just do it → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","An unknown cause behind a 22% reopen rate is a classic DMAIC candidate.","Unexplained variation between regions needs data and analysis to find the X’s — a good DMAIC project.","Several suspected but unproven causes need to be verified with data; this is DMAIC territory, not a just-do-it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-181",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Who should normally make up the **core team** named in a Green Belt project charter?",options:["Only senior managers, so that decisions can be made quickly","A small group of people who work in, or know, the process steps in scope, released part-time to the project","The whole department, so that everyone feels involved","Black Belts from other business units, so that the team stays independent of the process"],answer:1,hint:"The team needs process knowledge and enough time — not rank or head-count.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A charter names a **small, cross-functional team** of process experts — people who work in, supply or receive the steps in scope — released part-time, led by the Belt and supported by the Champion."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Managers alone lack hands-on process knowledge. A whole department is too large to run effective meetings. Outsiders cannot provide the process knowledge the team needs."}],final:"Small team of process experts, part-time → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Senior managers sponsor and remove barriers, but they rarely know the detailed process steps the team must map and measure.","","A whole department is far too large for a working team; stakeholders are kept informed rather than made members.","People outside the process cannot supply the hands-on process knowledge; Belts from elsewhere may advise, but they are not the core team."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-182",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Green Belt drafts four sentences for her charter on P2 incident SLA breaches. Which sentence belongs in the **business case**?",options:["From January to June, 22% of P2 incidents in the Muscat NOC breached the 4-hour restoration SLA","Reduce P2 SLA breaches from 22% to 8% by 30 June next year","The breaches cost about OMR 180,000 a year in SLA penalties and put the renewal of two enterprise contracts at risk","The project covers P2 incidents handled by the Muscat NOC; P1 incidents and field repairs are out of scope"],answer:2,hint:"Business case = why this project matters to the business now; problem = what is wrong; goal = target and date; scope = boundaries.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each charter element answers a different question. **Problem statement**: what is wrong, where, when, how big. **Goal**: the measurable target and deadline. **Scope**: what is in and out. **Business case**: why it matters to the business — money, customers, strategy."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only the sentence about **OMR 180,000** in penalties and contract renewals explains the business impact → business case."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The 22% sentence is the problem statement; “reduce … by 30 June” is the goal; “covers … out of scope” is the scope."}],final:"Financial and strategic impact → business case → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This describes what is wrong, where, when and how big — the problem statement, not the business case.","A measurable target with a deadline is the goal statement.","","In-scope and out-of-scope boundaries belong in the scope section."],trap:"Problem = symptom with numbers; business case = why the business should care.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.1"},{id:"sv-1-183",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Before writing the baseline into the charter, a Green Belt runs a run chart on **24 months** of the complaint-reopen rate:

\`\`\`
Run Chart of Reopen Rate (%)

Number of runs about median:        11     Number of runs up or down:       10
Expected number of runs:          13.0     Expected number of runs:      15.67
Longest run about median:            5     Longest run up or down:           3
Approx P-Value for Clustering:   0.202     Approx P-Value for Trends:    0.002
Approx P-Value for Mixtures:     0.798     Approx P-Value for Oscillation: 0.998
\`\`\`

What should she conclude?`,options:["The trends p-value (0.002) is below 0.05: the rate is drifting, so a simple 24-month average would misstate current performance — investigate and base the baseline on recent, representative data","All four p-values must be below 0.05 before there is a problem, so the 24-month average is a valid baseline","The oscillation p-value of 0.998 shows a strong non-random pattern, so the data cannot be used at all","The mixtures p-value of 0.798 proves the process is stable, so the 24-month average should be used"],answer:0,hint:"On a run chart, any of the four p-values below 0.05 signals a non-random pattern of that type.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Clustering 0.202, mixtures 0.798 and oscillation 0.998 are all above 0.05. **Trends = 0.002 < 0.05**: the rate is moving steadily up or down."},{title:"Key idea",body:"A baseline should describe the process as it **currently** runs. If the metric is trending, the 24-month mean mixes old and new performance. Find out why it is drifting, and use a recent, representative period."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Each p-value tests its own pattern, so one significant result is enough. A **high** p-value means **no** evidence of that pattern; it never “proves” stability."}],final:"Trends p = 0.002 → non-random drift → do not use the plain 24-month mean → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Each p-value tests a separate pattern; one significant pattern (trends) is enough to show non-random behaviour.","A high p-value means no evidence of oscillation; a signal needs a p-value below 0.05.","A high p-value is only an absence of evidence for mixtures; it cannot prove stability, and the trends test is significant."],trap:"Small p = signal; large p = no evidence — never “proof”.",bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 574"},{id:"sv-1-184",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"The problem is that **business fibre installations are late** against the CTQ of 5 working days from order acceptance. Which is the BEST **primary metric** for the project?",options:["Number of field technicians rostered per day","Annual savings in OMR","Customer Net Promoter Score (NPS)","Percentage of installations completed within 5 working days of order acceptance"],answer:3,hint:"The primary metric measures the project’s Y directly, in the CTQ’s own terms.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **primary metric** is the process measure the project is chartered to improve: the Y, defined to match the CTQ, with a baseline and a goal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The CTQ is “within 5 working days”, so the primary metric is the **percentage of installations completed within 5 working days**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Technician numbers are an input (X). Savings are the financial result. NPS is a broad, lagging measure driven by many factors — at most a secondary metric."}],final:"% on time against the CTQ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Technician rostering is a possible X (input), not the output the project must improve.","Savings are the financial benefit reported in the business case, not the process metric that measures the problem.","NPS is influenced by many things beyond installation time; it could be a secondary metric, not the primary one.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-185",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Finance will book savings only if the drop in **monthly contractor cost** (OMR thousands) is statistically supported. Nine months before and nine months after the change:

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: Before, After

Sample  N   Mean  StDev  SE Mean
Before  9  48.20   6.10      2.0
After   9  44.90   4.80      1.6

Difference = μ (Before) - μ (After)
Estimate for difference:  3.30
95% lower bound for difference:  -1.24
T-Test of difference = 0 (vs >): T-Value = 1.28  P-Value = 0.111  DF = 15
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, what should the Green Belt report?`,options:["Reject H0: the after-mean is 3.30 lower, so the savings are proven","Accept H0: the data prove the change saved nothing","Fail to reject H0: the data do not show a significant reduction yet, so the savings cannot be booked as verified — keep collecting data","The savings are verified, because the after-mean is lower than the before-mean"],answer:2,hint:"p < α → reject H0; p ≥ α → fail to reject H0 (never “accept”).",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"P-Value = **0.111**, which is above α = 0.05. The 95% lower bound for the difference is **−1.24**, so a difference of zero is still plausible."},{title:"Decide",body:"Fail to reject H0: there is not enough evidence that costs fell. The lower sample mean could be chance, so Finance cannot book verified hard savings yet."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A lower sample mean alone is not proof. And “accept H0” is wrong wording: failing to find a difference does not prove there is none."}],final:"p = 0.111 > 0.05 → fail to reject H0 → savings not yet verified → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Rejecting H0 needs p < 0.05; with p = 0.111 the 3.30 difference is not statistically significant.","“Accept H0” overstates the result: not finding a difference does not prove the saving is zero.","","Comparing two sample means by eye ignores sampling variation; the test says the gap could be chance."],trap:"Fail to reject ≠ accept; lower sample mean ≠ verified saving.",bookRef:"Book pp. 577–578"},{id:"sv-1-186",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A project frees **300 overtime hours a month** in the provisioning team. No overtime budget is removed. Staff use the freed time on a backlog of data-cleansing work. How should Finance classify this benefit?",options:["Hard savings","Soft savings (productivity / capacity gain)","Revenue growth","Not a benefit of any kind, so it must be left out of the report"],answer:1,hint:"Hard savings appear as a lower budget or spend on the P&L; freed capacity that is redeployed is soft.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Hard savings** reduce actual spend: a budget line goes down and the P&L shows it. **Soft savings** are real improvements — freed time, capacity, avoided future cost — that do not reduce today’s spend."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The overtime budget was not cut and the hours were redeployed, so no cash left the cost base. This is a **soft (productivity) saving**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It would become a hard saving only if the overtime budget were actually reduced. It is still worth reporting as soft benefit."}],final:"Freed capacity, no budget reduction → soft savings → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Hard savings need a real reduction in spend or budget; here the budget is unchanged and the hours were reused.","","No new sales or income arise from freed internal hours, so this is not revenue growth.","Freed capacity is a genuine benefit; it is reported as soft savings, not ignored."],bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 578"},{id:"sv-1-187",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A port-in request receives **45 minutes** of value-added work. Its total lead time is **2 calendar days** (48 hours). What is the **process cycle efficiency** (value-added time as a share of lead time)?",options:["1.56%","4.69%","22.5%","98.4%"],answer:0,hint:"PCE = value-added time ÷ total lead time, with both in the same units.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"VA time = 45 min. Lead time = $48\\times 60=2{,}880$ min."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{\\text{VA time}}{\\text{Lead time}}\\times 100\\%$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{45}{2880}\\times 100\\%=1.56\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Only about **1.6%** of the elapsed time adds value; the rest is mostly waiting — a typical Lean target."}],final:"PCE = 45 ÷ 2,880 = 1.56% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","4.69% treats 2 calendar days as two 8-hour working days (960 min), but the lead time was given as 48 elapsed hours.","22.5% divides 45 minutes by 2 days without converting to the same units.","98.4% is the non-value-added share (100% − 1.56%), not the efficiency."],bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-188",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"In a fibre-to-the-home order, all of the following are **value-added** steps EXCEPT:",options:["Splicing the fibre drop into the customer’s termination box","Configuring the router with the customer’s chosen plan","Checking the customer’s ID against the national register, as the regulator requires","Activating the service so the customer can go online"],answer:2,hint:"VA test: does the step change the form, fit or function of the product, and would the customer pay for it?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A step is **value-added** if it changes the form, fit or function of what the customer receives **and** the customer is willing to pay for it. Some non-value-added steps cannot be removed because a regulation requires them — these are **business value-added (BVA)**."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Splicing, configuring and activating all change what the customer gets. The ID check is required by the regulator, but it does not change the service and the customer would not pay for it → **BVA, not VA**."}],final:"Regulatory ID check = business value-added, not VA → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Splicing physically builds the connection the customer pays for — it changes the product’s form and function.","Configuring the router to the chosen plan changes the function of what the customer receives.","","Activation turns the connection into a working service — clearly value the customer pays for."],trap:"Required ≠ value-added: regulatory steps are BVA.",bookRef:"Book p. 117"},{id:"sv-1-189",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The term **“Lean production”** reached a wide audience through which **1990** book, based on an MIT study of the world car industry?",options:["Out of the Crisis","Quality Is Free","Toyota Production System: Beyond Large-Scale Production","The Machine That Changed the World"],answer:3,hint:"Womack, Jones and Roos compared Toyota with Western car makers in an MIT programme.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**The Machine That Changed the World** (Womack, Jones and Roos, 1990) reported the MIT International Motor Vehicle Program study. It made “Lean production” — the Toyota approach — known worldwide."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"**Out of the Crisis** is Deming’s quality-management book. **Quality Is Free** is Crosby’s book on the cost of quality. **Ohno’s** book describes TPS itself, but it did not give the method the name “Lean”."}],final:"MIT study, 1990 → The Machine That Changed the World → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Out of the Crisis is W. Edwards Deming’s book on quality management (the 14 points), not the source of the term Lean.","Quality Is Free is Philip Crosby’s book on the cost of quality, not the MIT car-industry study.","Taiichi Ohno’s book describes the Toyota Production System itself; the label “Lean” was popularised later by the MIT researchers.",""],trap:"TPS came from Toyota (Ohno); the name “Lean” came from the MIT study.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.4.2"},{id:"sv-1-190",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A provisioning team walks to a distant printer dozens of times a day. Everyone agrees on the cause (the layout) and the fix (move the printer and re-arrange two desks). The change is low-risk and can be done within a few days. Which approach fits BEST?",options:["A Kaizen event: a short, focused improvement workshop that implements the change","A full DMAIC project with hypothesis testing to prove the cause","A designed experiment (DOE) to optimise the desk positions","A Kaikaku: a radical redesign of the whole provisioning process"],answer:0,hint:"Match the tool to what is known: known cause and quick, low-risk fix → rapid Lean improvement.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **Kaizen event** is a short, focused burst of improvement, typically a few days. It suits problems with a clear cause and a practical, low-risk fix. DMAIC is for problems whose root cause is unknown and must be proven with data."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cause and fix are agreed, risk is low and the work takes days → **Kaizen event**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A DOE optimises process settings when several factors interact — overkill here. **Kaikaku** means radical, breakthrough change to the whole process, not a desk move."}],final:"Known cause, quick fix → Kaizen event → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","DMAIC is for unknown root causes; proving a cause everyone already agrees on wastes weeks.","A DOE is for optimising interacting process factors; a layout fix needs no experiment.","Kaikaku is radical, breakthrough redesign; this is a small, incremental change."],bookRef:"Book p. 510"},{id:"sv-1-191",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A Gemba walk on the order-to-activation process records these average minutes per order:

| Observation | Min per order |
|---|---|
| Order sits in a queue until the credit check returns | 40 |
| Agent re-keys order data already held in the CRM into the billing system | 20 |
| Agent corrects a wrong address flagged at dispatch | 25 |
| Supervisor gives a third approval after two approvals are already done | 15 |
| Technician re-configures a router shipped with the wrong profile | 30 |
| Agent walks to the shared scanner | 6 |

Which **waste** costs the most time per order?`,options:["Waiting","Correction","Overprocessing","Motion"],answer:1,hint:"Classify every row into one of the seven wastes first, then add the minutes for each waste.",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"Queue for credit check → **waiting** (40). Re-keying data → **overprocessing** (20). Correcting address → **correction** (25). Third approval → **overprocessing** (15). Re-configuring router → **correction** (30). Walking to scanner → **motion** (6)."},{title:"Add by waste",body:"Correction = 25 + 30 = **55**; waiting = 40; overprocessing = 20 + 15 = 35; motion = 6."},{title:"Decide",body:"Correction (rework) is the largest waste, even though the single biggest row is waiting."}],final:"Correction 55 min > waiting 40 > overprocessing 35 > motion 6 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Waiting is the largest single row (40), but correction totals 55 minutes once both rework rows are added.","","Overprocessing (re-keying + extra approval) totals 35 minutes — less than correction.","Motion is only the 6-minute walk to the scanner."],trap:"Add up by waste type; do not just pick the biggest row.",bookRef:"Book pp. 502–503"},{id:"sv-1-192",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"At the start of every job, field technicians walk around the van and the depot **looking for** the right splicing tool and test meter — about 12 minutes per job. Which waste is this?",options:["Conveyance","Waiting","Inventory","Motion"],answer:3,hint:"People moving or searching = motion; moving materials or information between steps = conveyance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Motion** is unnecessary movement of **people** — walking, reaching, searching. **Conveyance** is unnecessary movement of **materials, products or information** between process steps."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Technicians walking about and searching for tools → **motion**. 5S (straighten: a place for every tool) is the usual remedy."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing is being transported between steps, so it is not conveyance. The technicians are busy searching rather than idle and waiting, and no stock is piling up."}],final:"People searching and walking → motion → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Conveyance is moving materials or information between steps; here people are moving and searching.","Waiting is idle time until something arrives; the technicians are actively searching, not waiting for an input.","Inventory is excess stock or work in progress; the problem is finding tools, not holding too many.",""],trap:"Motion = people; conveyance = things.",bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-1-193",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Every morning the NOC spends **2 hours** compiling a 40-page performance report and emails it to **25 managers**. Only **3** of them asked for it, and they read only the summary page. Which waste BEST describes producing this report?",options:["Overprocessing","Inventory","Overproduction","Correction"],answer:2,hint:"Making more output than the customer demands, or before it is needed = overproduction.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Overproduction** means making more, sooner or faster than the customer needs. **Overprocessing** means doing more work **on** an item than the customer requires — extra steps, extra checks or extra precision."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Copies go to 22 managers who never asked for them → output with no demand → **overproduction**. This is the waste that also creates inventory and waiting."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Overprocessing would fit if each requested report carried extra polish nobody needs. Here the main waste is producing output for people who do not want it."}],final:"Output nobody demanded → overproduction → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Overprocessing is adding unneeded work to an item that is wanted; the core problem here is producing reports that are not wanted at all.","Inventory is stock or work in progress waiting to be used; the report is not stored for later use.","","Correction is fixing errors; nothing in the stem is being reworked."],trap:"Overproduction (too much output) vs overprocessing (too much work on one output).",bookRef:"Book pp. 502–503"},{id:"sv-1-194",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A NOC summarises a year of monthly 5S audits (score 0–5 per S):

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Audit score by S

S                 N   Mean
Sort             12    4.4
Straighten       12    4.1
Shine            12    3.9
Standardize      12    3.6
Self-discipline  12    1.9
\`\`\`

What does this pattern MOST likely show?`,options:["The first four S’s were put in place, but the habit is not being sustained — strengthen self-discipline with regular audits, leader follow-up and accountability","Sort is the weak point, so the team should restart with a new red-tag exercise","Shine is the main problem, so cleaning frequency should be doubled","The 5S programme is complete, because four of the five scores are above 3.5"],answer:0,hint:"Find the lowest mean, then recall what that S is for.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Sort to Standardize score 3.6–4.4. **Self-discipline** is far lower at **1.9**."},{title:"Key idea",body:"The fifth S (self-discipline / sustain) makes 5S a habit through audits, management follow-up and accountability. A low fifth S means the gains are likely to slip back."}],final:"Lowest score is Self-discipline (1.9) → sustain is failing → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Sort has the highest mean (4.4); it is the strongest S, not the weak point.","Shine scores 3.9, well above Self-discipline (1.9); doubling cleaning does not address the real gap.","A 1.9 on Self-discipline shows the programme is not sustained; 5S is never “complete”."],bookRef:"Book p. 508"},{id:"sv-1-195",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing team applies 5S to its shared drive. After deleting obsolete files, it sets up a **fixed folder structure and a file-naming convention** so that anyone can find any file within 30 seconds. Which 5S step is this?",options:["Sort","Straighten (Set in order)","Shine","Standardize"],answer:1,hint:"A place for everything, and everything in its place.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Straighten (set in order)** gives every needed item a defined, labelled place so it can be found and returned quickly — “a place for everything and everything in its place”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Deleting obsolete files was **Sort**. Creating a fixed folder structure and naming convention for fast retrieval is **Straighten**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Shine is cleaning and inspecting. Standardize writes the agreed routines down so that every team does the first three S’s the same way."}],final:"Defined locations and labels for fast retrieval → Straighten → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Sort is removing what is not needed — the obsolete files deleted before this step.","","Shine is cleaning and inspecting (for files, fixing broken links or duplicates), not designing where things live.","Standardize turns the first three S’s into written, shared routines; designing the folder layout itself is Straighten."],trap:"Delete = Sort; a home for each item = Straighten; make it the rule = Standardize.",bookRef:"Book p. 506"}];export{e as items};
